WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Film Courage: If a writer has three ideas,&nbsp;
how do they know which one to choose?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.120
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting Teacher and&nbsp;
Consultant: I think it depends on what they want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.800
out of the writing project. I think there are a&nbsp;
few criteria you can look to and it's a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.660
personal decision. I also think you don't ever&nbsp;
have to throw an idea away if you have three that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.980
you want to write, that means you're really just&nbsp;
choosing which one you're going to write next and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:30.900
then you can always have the other two on deck. I&nbsp;
personally think that you should gauge the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.880
and make sure that each idea you're weighing&nbsp;
is a strong enough idea to be a screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:42.360
that's the first thing. But if all that's equal&nbsp;
I think you should look at what you want to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:52.140
out of the project. Is it helping you learn a new&nbsp;
skill in your writing toolkit? Is it helping you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:57.720
advance your career in a particular way? Is it&nbsp;
filling out your portfolio of writing samples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:07.080
so that is helpful to gauge what do I want this to&nbsp;
do for me so that no writing is wasted, it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.740
wasted time but so that you're getting the most&nbsp;
out of of the project that you're going to write.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.820
Then also if the ending I&nbsp;
think that's very helpful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.500
because that sometimes if you start a&nbsp;
writing project without knowing the ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:31.380
that can be a longer, more frustrating process to&nbsp;
to outline that. But if you how this story ends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.080
the outlining of it, the figuring out how&nbsp;
the the plot progresses all the steps in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.860
plot and everything that's a little bit easier&nbsp;
because what destination you're headed towards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.200
so that's interesting. When you said that you've&nbsp;
actually outlined for people once you see their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.960
finished script if you see someone who's hung&nbsp;
up on the ending, do you get them to that point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:56.820
and then say okay now I mean I realize you're&nbsp;
not figuring out the ending for them but do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.460
outline up until that point and then maybe that&nbsp;
helps them through it? I'm making an outline from&nbsp;&nbsp;

